# THE LAYERS THAT STATE A REMAINDER OF THEIR OWN, WITH THE MAGNITUDE THEY PUT ON IT. One row per
# layer filing the first branch of the `StatedRemainder` wrapper -- the sign, the buffer it names
# as absorber, and the quantity if the filer gave one.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE POSITIVE HALF OF layers/denied_remainders.sqlc, AND IT HAD TO BE NAMED BEFORE THE
#    ANTI-JOIN COULD BE READ. Three separate call sites reached past both relations into
#    `pm.layer` for `qty_low/mode/high/unit` -- arithmetic/filed_remainder.sqlc, matrices.sqlc
#    and queries/matrices/4-converted-remainder.sqlc -- and one of them wrote
#    `WHERE remainder_absent IS NULL`, which is the NOT its sibling already states positively.
#
# ⛔⛔ `qty_unit` WAS READ BY NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY. The filed remainder is compared against
#    a derived `n - d` in two places and neither reads the unit the filer wrote beside the
#    number. Carrying it here is what makes that comparison askable at all.
#
# ⭐ THE QUANTITY IS ITSELF A TYPED ABSENCE. A layer may state that it HAS a remainder -- naming
#   its sign and its absorber -- and decline to size it. `qty_absent` is that filing, and it is a
#   different document from one that denies the remainder outright.
-- pm:Layer/pm:remainder taking the pm:claim branch of pm:StatedRemainder.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.sign, l.sign_absent,
       l.absorber_taxonomy, l.absorber_value,
       l.qty_low, l.qty_mode, l.qty_high, l.qty_unit, l.qty_absent
FROM pm.layer l
WHERE l.remainder_absent IS NULL
